#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176753} :activity/id #uuid "a82f82dc-e433-5288-bb71-1269a1685809" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048516 :topic/id #uuid "a5effb25-537a-5891-b350-6a8d3f357154" :topic/math-academy-id 3642} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175958 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175804 :question/id #uuid "5e71fcd7-b782-5399-979f-fd62607b5653" :question/math-academy-id "e-7421"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33fca834-0063-5252-832f-5efb4be6e599" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382922}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions Using the Properties of Expected Value"} :step/id #uuid "7c87517d-0124-503b-ad8d-9d75e2bcf4c6" :step/math-academy-id 27507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175991 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175842 :question/id #uuid "46566681-c2e4-53e1-83c4-d438cabc8066" :question/math-academy-id "e-9106"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c105dd8-6739-5889-a529-9182ea814d76" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048317}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367212}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Moment of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "e4a2022f-0bfe-53a7-a3a3-924b5ed19a7c" :step/math-academy-id 33947 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176751}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177003 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over $S.$\n\nThe values $\\textrm E[X]$ and $\\textrm E[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment**, respectively.\n\nIt's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm E[X^3]$ is the **third moment.**\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x)$ is the first moment\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X^2] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^2 \\cdot f(x)$ is the second moment\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X^3] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^3 \\cdot f(x)$ is the third moment\n- $\\,\\cdots$\n\nIn general, the $n$th moment is given by\n\n$$\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X^n] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^n \\cdot f(x).$$\n\nThe concept of a moment will become important later. In particular, we'll soon see how $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ can be used to compute the **variance** of a random variable.\n\nFinally, note that $\\textrm E[X], \\textrm E[X^2], \\textrm E[X^3],$ etc. are sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moment.\n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." :tutorial/id #uuid "0f377288-40af-50ed-b77c-90edf07bb005" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5499 :tutorial/title "The Moments of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "77279341-1e22-5bb1-bd2c-8981a3235685" :step/math-academy-id 34228 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177004 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a discrete random variable $X$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of $X,$ denoted $\\textrm E[X],$ is given by\n\n$$\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x),$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is the probability mass function of $X.$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the expected value of the random variable $X^2$ as\n\n$$\\textrm E[X^2] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^2 \\cdot f(x).$$\n\nFor example, suppose that the random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nIn this case, $\\textrm E[X^2]$ is given by\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x) \\\\ & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.25 \\\\ & =1\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.5+9\\cdot 0.25 \\\\ & =4.5.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Watch out!** When computing $\\textrm E[X^2],$ it's important to remember that we still evaluate the probability mass function as $f(x),$ *not* $f(x^2).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "544551ff-1bd9-5834-8bed-084fe6b81425" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5503 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e5dae560-1167-5534-8639-30d7fa5c56b5" :step/math-academy-id 34238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176741}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176042 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175689 :question/id #uuid "69fdace0-5c5b-519b-b03b-40fd523915f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10128"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebfe6309-b8a3-5e4c-b2ae-495a91bcc433" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410426}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Higher Moments of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "f44e66b4-ae69-5a8a-b61a-38a70551aa2f" :step/math-academy-id 38212 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176685}}] :activity/title "Moments of Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176159}]}
